"Check the ledger to reconcile the lit nights with the oil spent.","conflict":"Toll deflects her, insists an unentered night did not happen, and uses his writ to bar her from the ledger.","outcome":"Wenna confirms the page is gone and that Toll is guarding it; Toll resolves to keep her watched and her questions quiet.","depends_on":["scn_oil_store"]}]},{"id":"act_two_rising","title":"What the Bog Keeps","purpose":"rising_action","scenes":[{"id":"scn_toll_writ","title":"The Reeve's Writ","summary":"Toll issues a writ to call in the oil tithe early from the turf-cutters, framing it as the cure for the shortfall and a way to keep the parish calm and fed.","location_id":"loc_moot_kirk","character_ids":["chr_reeve_toll"],"goal":"Keep the dark night buried through one more midwinter by keeping the parish busy with the tithe.","conflict":"The call-in will fall hardest on the turf-cutters and may drive someone to speak, which Toll's own conscience about the cut page makes harder to accept.","outcome":"The writ goes out to the Bog Hollow, and Toll marks the turf-cutters, especially those who were out on the missing night, as a risk.","depends_on":["scn_ledger_page"]},{"id":"scn_bog_edge","title":"The Bog Line","summary":"Wenna walks to the edge of the Bog Hollow by day, meets Lob Ather, and finds him frightened of the call-in and of her questions. He denies knowing anything.","location_id":"loc_bog_hollow","character_ids":["chr_wenna_crowe","chr_lob_ather"],"goal":"Find a witness to the night the page might hold.","conflict":"Lob's fear makes him lie first, and Wenna wants proof, which the bog does not give.","outcome":"Lob lets slip that he was past the bog line on a night last winter, then retreats; Wenna now has a thread to follow.","depends_on":["scn_toll_writ"]},{"id":"scn_wardens_croft","title":"The Wardens' Silence","summary":"Back at the croft Wenna confronts the wardens' guild over the oil count; they blame the council and say the turf-cutters over-claim, each side blaming the other for the silence.","location_id":"loc_keepers_croft","character_ids":["chr_wenna_crowe"],"goal":"Learn from her own guild what happened on the missing night.","conflict":"The wardens will not name the night, and her need for proof means she cannot simply accept their vague fear.","outcome":"She understands the guild and the council both fear the same night, and decides to return to Lob after dusk-lighting.","depends_on":["scn_bog_edge"]},{"id":"scn_dusk_lob","title":"The Night Visit","summary":"After lighting the wall, Wenna finds Lob at the Bog Hollow's edge at dusk, where the lights drown and voices carry wrong. He nearly tells her what he saw, but the bog's false lights and voices break his nerve.","location_id":"loc_bog_hollow","character_ids":["chr_wenna_crowe","chr_lob_ather"],"goal":"Persuade Lob to say what he saw on the missing night.","conflict":"The bog's wrong voices, the rule against carrying lanterns past the bog line, and his fear of the reeve stop him; Wenna cannot get proof from what she hears.","outcome":"Lob admits the wall itself went dark while he was out past the line, and he begs her to say nothing; Wenna must now choose between his safety and the truth.","depends_on":["scn_wardens_croft"]},{"id":"scn_reeve_seizure","title":"The Seizure","summary":"Toll's writ seizes oil from Lob's hearth after word of Wenna's visits reaches him, and Toll confronts Wenna at the Lantern Wall, threatening to bar her from the wick bench.","location_id":"loc_lantern_wall","character_ids":["chr_wenna_crowe","chr_reeve_toll","chr_lob_ather"],"goal":"Wenna wants to protect Lob's oil and the wall's supply; Toll wants to stop her questions.","conflict":"Toll's writ outruns her objections and Lob, pressed by fear, lies and denies everything, undermining her.","outcome":"Lob's oil is taken and Wenna is warned off, but his lie shows her he is terrified, and she resolves to read the truth herself on the night the wall is tested.","depends_on":["scn_dusk_lob"]}]},{"id":"act_three_climax","title":"Midwinter","purpose":"climax","scenes":[{"id":"scn_midwinter_wall","title":"The Wall Tested","summary":"On midwinter night the oil runs short and the lanterns gutter one by one. Wenna races the wall with her wick-craft to keep one wick burning while Toll tries to have her removed.","location_id":"loc_lantern_wall","character_ids":["chr_wenna_crowe","chr_reeve_toll"],"goal":"Wenna must keep at least one wick burning; Toll must keep the dark night buried and the parish orderly.","conflict":"The oil is short, the wind is high, and Toll's writ orders the wardens to stand down while the lamps fail.","outcome":"Wenna keeps a single wick alive by her skill, and she sends for Lob and the council to meet at the kirk before dawn.","depends_on":["scn_reeve_seizure"]},{"id":"scn_moot_reading","title":"The Moot","summary":"At the Moot Kirk before the council, Lob, shaking, finally says aloud what he saw, and Wenna names the cut page; Toll is made to account for it in front of the moot he deceived to save.","location_id":"loc_moot_kirk","character_ids":["chr_wenna_crowe","chr_reeve_toll","chr_lob_ather"],"goal":"Wenna wants the truth of the dark night spoken before the council; Toll wants to keep it buried.","conflict":"Toll's writ and gravity press the moot to silence Lob, who must overcome his fear and his flaw of lying first.","outcome":"Lob tells what he saw, the council hears that the wall went dark on the missing night and what the parish did about it, and Toll can no longer keep the page buried.","depends_on":["scn_midwinter_wall"]}]},{"id":"act_four_resolution","title":"The Lit Ledger","purpose":"resolution","scenes":[{"id":"scn_toll_reckoning","title":"The Reeve's Reckoning","summary":"With the truth spoken, Toll's cut page is read back to him and he admits he cut it to save the parish. The council strips his writ and bids him enter the dark night into the ledger himself.","location_id":"loc_moot_kirk","character_ids":["chr_reeve_toll","chr_wenna_crowe"],"goal":"Toll must answer for the page; Wenna wants the night entered in the ledger so the rite is honest.","conflict":"Toll cannot forgive himself for how well the lie worked, and the council is divided on whether to punish him or keep the peace.","outcome":"The night is entered in the ledger, Toll loses his writ but is spared exile, and the council agrees to reopen the oil tithe on honest terms.","depends_on":["scn_moot_reading"]},{"id":"scn_hearth_oil","title":"Oil Returned","summary":"Lob's seized oil is returned and the turf-cutters bring their tithe to the croft under the new honest count, while Wenna and Lob talk about what the bog gave and took.","location_id":"loc_keepers_croft","character_ids":["chr_wenna_crowe","chr_lob_ather"],"goal":"Restock the oil store and settle the tithe between the turf-cutters and the wardens.","conflict":"Lob fears the parish's judgment for his silence, and Wenna still cannot prove whether the rite is sacred or superstition.","outcome":"The store is filled enough for the winter and Lob is forgiven by his neighbours; Wenna accepts that she may never have proof and keeps the rite anyway.","depends_on":["scn_toll_reckoning"]},{"id":"scn_last_dusk","title":"Keep the Wick","summary":"At dusk Wenna lights the wall once more, every lantern burning, with Lob lighting the last wick beside her at the wall.","location_id":"loc_lantern_wall","character_ids":["chr_wenna_crowe","chr_lob_ather"],"goal":"Light the whole wall honestly and keep the lantern rite through the rest of winter.","conflict":"The bog's wrong voices still carry across the moor, and Wenna must keep her flame steady without proof of what answers.","outcome":"The wall burns whole through the night, the parish has changed from silence to witness, and Wenna keeps the wick as her own.","depends_on":["scn_hearth_oil"]}]}]}
